# One JDK for everyone, from the build: nothing depends on the machine's java.
common --enable_bzlmod
# Bytecode is Java 21; tests RUN on JDK 25, the JDK the gates have always run on.
# The two differ observably: BigDecimal.valueOf(NaN)'s message, which the PCT
# expected-failure pins record (Test_LegendLite_EssentialFunctions_PCT).
build --java_language_version=21
build --java_runtime_version=remotejdk_25
build --tool_java_language_version=21
# Tools run on the same JDK as the tests: java_run (//tools/java_run) runs the
# generators' TARGET-configuration jars — compiled against JDK 25's class library —
# on this runtime, so it must be one they were compiled for.
build --tool_java_runtime_version=remotejdk_25
# the engine's test clock (legend-engine minted its goldens in GMT) — one clock, everywhere.
# Not on Windows: there DuckDB, and any JVM not started through junit_test (which sets
# -Duser.timezone=GMT itself: tools/junit/defs.bzl), read the system's zone and ignore TZ, so a
# test that compares zoned text sets its session's zone itself (WarehouseJdbcTest, 2026-10-02).
test --test_env=TZ=GMT

# Windows: tests read the upstream trees and sibling modules through RUNFILES by
# relative path, so runfiles must exist as a directory (Windows defaults to a
# manifest only), and Bazel must create real symlinks (CI runs as admin).
common --enable_platform_specific_config
startup --windows_enable_symlinks
build:windows --enable_runfiles
# Windows: bash, for what still needs one -- rules_jvm_external's Maven fetch (it fails
# without BAZEL_SH), rules_js's launchers and the genrules. Bazel looks for one in BAZEL_SH,
# then MSYS2's registry key, then PATH: on a desk with Git for Windows and no MSYS2, from a
# PowerShell or cmd prompt, it found none and every Maven repository failed to fetch
# (2026-10-02). So it is named: Git for Windows' own bash, at Git's default install path.
# Git installed elsewhere, or MSYS2's bash wanted? These lines win over Bazel's own search,
# so repeat both in your ~/.bazelrc with that path; the later line wins. Without that, the
# first shell action fails naming the missing bash.exe (review of #14, 2026-10-03).
common:windows --repo_env="BAZEL_SH=C:/Program Files/Git/usr/bin/bash.exe"
build:windows --shell_executable="C:/Program Files/Git/usr/bin/bash.exe"

# CI: `--config=ci` — every test's log on failure, and no progress-bar noise.
common:ci --color=no --curses=no --show_progress_rate_limit=30
test:ci --test_output=errors --test_summary=terse
# CI's HEAPS. A JVM defaults to a quarter of the machine's RAM: plenty on a desk
# (which keeps that default, so local loops are not capped), too little on a hosted
# runner. Pinned here, not on the targets, from Maven's measured peaks (2026-09-09):
# a PCT suite's Pure graph is a 2.0-2.8 GB live set and five in one JVM need 8 GB;
# the Pure PAR generator (a build tool) peaks at ~2.5 GB. The first Bazel CI run
# (2026-09-23) died out of heap on Linux gate 6 and macOS gates 7/9 without them.
build:ci --jvmopt=-Xmx8g
build:ci --host_jvmopt=-Xmx3g
# The 7 GB macOS runner: one test JVM at a time (gates-run.yml), each suite in its
# own JVM, 4 GB each. Given after --config=ci: the JVM takes the LAST -Xmx.
build:ci-small --jvmopt=-Xmx4g

# LOCAL SCHEDULING (2026-09-27, measured on the 10-core / 32 GB desk — GATES.md entry of
# that date): Bazel's own estimate of a test's memory comes from `size` (an `enormous`
# test is assumed to need 800 MB) while our JVM lanes peak at 0.7-8.2 GB, so with one
# test per core it ran ten fat JVMs at once and every lane's time was 3-7x its own.
# The heavy lanes declare their MEASURED peak plus headroom (`resources:memory:<MB>`
# tags on the targets) and the machine declares its budget: 60% of RAM for tests
# leaves the Bazel server, the OS and whatever else runs on the desk the rest. Bazel
# then packs tests by memory. CI keeps its own caps (--config=ci / ci-small).
test --local_resources=memory=HOST_RAM*.6
# A local disk cache: a fresh worktree or a `bazel clean` costs seconds, not core's
# compile; CI passes the same path explicitly (gates-run.yml).
build --disk_cache=~/.cache/bazel-disk

# rules_js depends on aspect_tools_telemetry, which reports ruleset usage from
# every build unless told not to. This repository's builds send nothing.
common --repo_env=ASPECT_TOOLS_TELEMETRY=-all
common --repo_env=DO_NOT_TRACK=1
